From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 17:47:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ether: let the Connectivity Engine read its own configuration

CNE is the component that tells the RIL which data technology to prefer, and it is the only path
by which IWLAN becomes a candidate at all. On this port it cannot read a single one of its own
properties:

    avc: denied { read } comm="cnd" tcontext=u:object_r:default_prop:s0
    avc: denied { read } comm="cnd" tcontext=u:object_r:wifi_hal_prop:s0

persist.cne.feature is set to 1 and cnd never sees it, because every persist.cne.* name falls
through to default_prop, which cnd is not allowed to read. wifi.interface -- which libcne reads to
find the WLAN interface it is meant to be evaluating -- is wifi_hal_prop, also denied.

The consequence is visible one layer up, in the RIL:

    qcril_qmi_nas_update_data_rte: .. pref data tech UNKNOWN, is current 0
    qcril_qmi_nas_update_data_rte: preferred data tech available UNKNOWN
    qcril_qmi_nas_update_data_rte:  .. prep CDMA / prep EVDO / prep GSM / prep LTE

CNE offers no preferred data technology, and IWLAN never appears among the candidates it prepares,
so nothing ever asks the modem for an ePDG tunnel. That is the AP-side end of the Wi-Fi calling
path: the modem accepts the configuration and then has nothing to act on.

Give the prefixes cnd and libcne actually read their own type rather than widening default_prop,
which would hand cnd read access to every unlabelled property on the system.

persist.data.iwlan.* is deliberately left alone: netmgrd and qmuxd read those and are not denied
default_prop, so relabelling would take away access they currently have.

This is the same failure as the bluetooth.core.le.vendor_capabilities.enabled entry above it -- a
property whose reader cannot see it, so the setting silently has no effect.

Not claiming this alone brings up VoWiFi. persist.vendor.cnd.iwlan and persist.vendor.cnd.wqe are
both read by libcne and both unset; they are the obvious next step, but they are worth nothing
until CNE can read that part of the property space at all.
---
 sepolicy/cnd.te            | 12 ++++++++++++
 sepolicy/property.te       |  1 +
 sepolicy/property_contexts | 15 +++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 28 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 sepolicy/cnd.te

diff --git a/sepolicy/cnd.te b/sepolicy/cnd.te
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..29bd066
--- /dev/null
+++ b/sepolicy/cnd.te
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# The Connectivity Engine is the component that tells the RIL which data technology to prefer, and
+# it is the only path by which IWLAN becomes a candidate at all. It reads two sets of properties and
+# is denied both:
+#
+#   avc: denied { read } comm="cnd" tcontext=u:object_r:default_prop:s0
+#   avc: denied { read } comm="cnd" tcontext=u:object_r:wifi_hal_prop:s0
+#
+# The first is its own configuration -- persist.cne.feature above all, plus persist.vendor.cnd.iwlan
+# and persist.vendor.cnd.wqe -- now carried by vendor_cne_prop. The second is wifi.interface, which
+# libcne reads to find the WLAN interface it is supposed to be evaluating.
+get_prop(cnd, vendor_cne_prop)
+get_prop(cnd, wifi_hal_prop)
diff --git a/sepolicy/property.te b/sepolicy/property.te
index d3b31be..5c1b515 100644
--- a/sepolicy/property.te
+++ b/sepolicy/property.te
@@ -1,2 +1,3 @@
 type audio_smartamp_init_prop, property_type;
 type vendor_timekeep_prop, property_type;
+type vendor_cne_prop, property_type;
diff --git a/sepolicy/property_contexts b/sepolicy/property_contexts
index c9e974f..ba770ba 100644
--- a/sepolicy/property_contexts
+++ b/sepolicy/property_contexts
@@ -9,3 +9,18 @@ persist.vendor.timeadjust u:object_r:vendor_timekeep_prop:s0
 #           "bluetooth.core.le.vendor_capabilities.enabled"
 # so the gate silently defaulted to true and the APCF crash persisted.
 bluetooth.core.le.vendor_capabilities.enabled    u:object_r:bluetooth_config_prop:s0 exact bool
+
+# CNE's own configuration. Every one of these falls through to default_prop, which cnd is not
+# allowed to read, so the Connectivity Engine cannot see its own master switch:
+#   avc: denied { read } comm="cnd" tcontext=u:object_r:default_prop:s0
+# persist.cne.feature is set to 1 and cnd never sees it. The visible consequence is at the RIL:
+#   qcril_qmi_nas_update_data_rte: .. pref data tech UNKNOWN
+#   qcril_qmi_nas_update_data_rte:  .. prep CDMA / EVDO / GSM / LTE
+# -- CNE offers no preferred data technology and IWLAN is never among the candidates, so nothing
+# ever asks the modem to try an ePDG tunnel.
+#
+# Only the prefixes cnd and libcne actually read are moved. persist.data.iwlan.* is deliberately
+# left in default_prop: netmgrd and qmuxd read those and are not denied it, so relabelling would
+# take away access they currently have.
+persist.cne.            u:object_r:vendor_cne_prop:s0
+persist.vendor.cnd.     u:object_r:vendor_cne_prop:s0
